messages.content:
{
 "what_you_observe": [
  "Tuesday, February 2. Seven pm.",
  "Phil said in the lobby: Loudly, to no one in particular, as the door swings shut behind him: \"I've got a couch to move on the other side of the river. Don't wait up.\"",
  "Rita records Alma at the Rialto and puts her recording on the patch.",
  "Larry said in the lobby: I say to whoever's still at the table: 'I'm taking the Rialto. You guys cover the ballroom tonight.' I don't wait for an answer. I'm already at the door with the bag over my shoulder.",
  "Larry records Alma on his last charged take. The reel stays with him.",
  "You are now at Rialto.",
  "There is time for one evening visit. The ballroom lights are on and the station line is open."
 ],
 "your_own_memories": [
  {
   "what_you_did": "Rita records Alma at the Rialto and puts her recording on the patch.",
   "what_you_said": "Ballroom's yours. I'm on the Rialto. One mic, one cable, a chair if her back's bad. I'll handle the rest.",
   "your_intention": "Be in the Rialto booth before the last reel loads onto the regional line. Sit beside Alma, not across. Let her talk. Record it. Send the file to Pittsburgh tonight with no title, no slug, no broadcast package. One voice, one daughter, one machine about to stop."
  }
 ],
 "who_you_are_with": "your present location",
 "physical_choices": {
  "transmit_interview": "Take the regional line at the station and send any usable interview with Alma to her daughter.",
  "go_dancing": "Spend the evening at the ballroom, open to dancing with a colleague.",
  "go_to_party_for_work": "Cover the party with your notebook, without dancing.",
  "take_evening_off": "Choose a quiet evening on your own."
 }
}

{
  "action": "transmit_interview",
  "detail": "I pull the folding chair up to the station line, the one with the green LED still breathing. I plug the patch cable into the terminal, the same one I used twenty minutes ago when I was sitting on the floor beside Alma's knees and she was telling me about the year the carbon arc went out during *Lawrence of Arabia* and she'd walked the length of the corridor with a flashlight and a roll of splicing tape. I dial Pittsburgh. I don't look at the call log. I press transmit. The file goes. No title. No slug. No producer credit. Just the waveform, her voice, the faint tick of the booth's ventilation, and the last thirty seconds where she says *tell her the reel count is in the drawer under the left armrest, the one with the rust, she'll know which one* and then stops, and I let the silence run out instead of cutting it. I let the line close. I unplug the cable. I put the chair back against the wall. I stand there in the dark of the booth with the projector reel still warm on the shelf above me, and I do not go to the ballroom. I do not open the notebook. I take my coat off the hook by the door and put it back on, and I walk out the side exit into the February air, and I am done for the night.",
  "intention": "Get her voice to Pittsburgh before I sleep. Clean. No package. No title. Just her, saying the things only her daughter needs to hear, out of the mouth of the woman who ran the machine that played them. One transmission. One daughter. Done.",
  "words": "To the line, to no one, quiet, as the file loads: \"No slug. No title. Her name, her voice, the girl's number. That's the whole thing. Send it clean.\""
}
